*Application for Assistant Professor in Artificial Intelligence for Manufacturing Systems, 77978-0526*

I am writing to apply for the *Assistant Professor in Artificial Intelligence for Manufacturing Systems* post (*77978-0526*) in the *Advanced Manufacturing Processes and Systems (AMPS) Research Group* at WMG, University of Warwick. I am currently a Research Assistant at the *Power Electronics and Machines Centre (PEMC), University of Nottingham*, having completed my *PhD in Electrical Engineering at Southeast University (SEU), Nanjing*, in September 2025 with the dissertation _Multi-physics Analysis and Design of Hairpin Winding PM Traction Machines in Electric Vehicles_. My research has developed AI-driven multi-physics design, digital-twin modelling and advanced manufacturing methods for high power-density electrical machines, and I would be glad to contribute to AMPS's research and teaching at the intersection of artificial intelligence, digital manufacturing, and intelligent industrial systems.

A central thread of my work is *AI/ML for manufacturing*, anchored by my ICEM 2026 paper _A Deep Learning Based Magnetic Field Surrogate Modeling Method for Interior Permanent Magnet Machine in EVs_. The paper introduces an *implicit physics-knowledge embedding* approach in which a deep neural network learns to map machine design parameters and rotor position to the full *magnetic vector potential distribution*, with a Fourier feature layer for the spatial coordinates. Conventional post-processing then recovers torque, flux linkage and iron loss. The model reaches *R#super[2] = 0.9996* for average torque and *0.9730* for iron loss on an unseen validation set, while reducing inference time by *more than 94%* against direct finite-element analysis. The surrogate sits on an *in-house Python finite-element code package* I have developed, covering electromagnetic, structural-modal and mechanical-stress analyses. Together they form a *physics-grounded digital twin* of the machine that supports rapid what-if studies, design-space exploration and AI-assisted optimisation, and is currently being applied to magnet-free synchronous machine work at PEMC. This combination of physics-based simulation, deep-learning surrogates and open, scriptable software is directly relevant to AMPS's interests in digital twins, manufacturing analytics and software-enabled industrial systems.

My research record to date includes *22 journal papers, of which 17 are in IEEE Transactions* (across _IEEE Trans. Industrial Electronics_, _Transportation Electrification_, _Industry Applications_, and _Energy Conversion_), 18 peer-reviewed conference papers at IEEE IEMDC, IEEE ECCE, ICEM and ICEMS, and 9 patents. The manufacturing-engineering content includes *novel n-layer flat-wire and hairpin winding design* with conductor-layout optimisation and tolerance-aware mechanical design, *high-fidelity lumped-parameter and fluid-solid coupled oil-cooling models* validated against prototype measurements, and *multi-objective multi-physics optimisation* coupling electromagnetic, thermal and mechanical objectives. This work has been recognised by the *2025 IEEE TEC Electric Vehicle Design Competition (1#super[st] Prize)*, the *2025 IEEE Trans. Transportation Electrification Prize Paper Award (2#super[nd] Prize)*, the *1#super[st] Prize in the IEEE IAS CMD Thesis Contest (non-PhD category)*, the *CIEEC Best Paper Award (2023)*, and the *Jiangsu Province Outstanding Master Degree Thesis Award (2022)*. I also serve as *Co-Organiser and Co-Chair of an ICEM 2026 Special Session* on _Next-Generation Electrified Transport_ in Funchal, Madeira, Portugal.

I have also gained substantive *industrial development experience* through a multi-year collaboration with *Inovance*, a world-leading EV traction-machine supplier, on two oil-cooled PM traction machines: an *18 000 rpm 113 kW A0-class machine* (138 Nm, 7.78 kW/kg, 95.3% peak efficiency) and a *20 000 rpm 270 kW A-class machine* (380 Nm, 7.87 kW/kg, 97.4% peak efficiency). Each was taken through a full industrial cycle of multi-physics design, engineering drawings and tolerance specification for high-reliability manufacture, and AVL dynamometer characterisation. This combined academic and industrial background should support future funding applications across UK research councils and industrial partners.

I would be glad to contribute to teaching across WMG's portfolio at undergraduate, MSc, Executive Education and Masterclass level, in areas such as *AI for manufacturing engineers, finite-element principles and FEA-based design, digital-twin laboratories, multi-physics machine design, and embedded systems for project-based learning*. During my doctoral studies I served as teaching assistant on the SEU course _Integrated Practice of Electrical Machine Systems_, preparing tutorial material, leading Q&A, reviewing project proposals, and contributing mark recommendations. My toolchain spans MATLAB/Simulink, ANSYS Maxwell and Mechanical, Python and PyTorch, and C/C++ for TI C2000 and STM32, complemented by hands-on power-electronics prototyping. I would also be glad to undertake *PCAPP* and *Higher Education Academy Fellowship* in due course.

For background, my doctoral supervisor, *Prof. Zhongze Wu*, held a Research Fellow position in electrical machines at WMG, University of Warwick from January 2017 to August 2018, which has given me a long-standing familiarity with WMG's research culture through his mentorship.

My CV and detailed responses to the Person Specification are attached. I would be glad to discuss my research and teaching plans further at interview.

